
The composables android declares are ordinary components, so a program can
call one directly to reach a composable no stdlib wrapper covers. The generic
emitter reads the declaration: the #[kt.native] name is the composable's name,
each declared prop is the Compose argument of that name in declaration order, and children are a trailing lambda. A childless composable takes no
lambda, and a #id carried onto one becomes a testTag.

`then` appends Modifiers the Style has no field for, built from the calls
android.sngl declares -- `android.modifier.fillMaxSize()` -- so every name in
them is imported through the screen's import set. The window's own content is
kept out from under the status bar and the display cutout, last in the chain,
so it insets the content rather than the background. Text declares
`modifier` and `style` separately, so the Style behind the Modifier and the one
behind the TextStyle are distinct props.

The stdlib wrappers reach the same emitter: hbox's android override is written
in terms of Row and scroll's adds a `then` entry, and both inline to the
composable, so neither name survives into codegen. stack layers its children, so
it is a Box and not a Column.
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import android "sngl:platform/android"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main node {
    android.Column(then=[android.modifier.fillMaxSize()]) {
        android.Row #bar(modifier={padding=4px}) {
            android.Spacer(modifier={height=8px})
            android.Text(text="hi", modifier={padding=1px}, style={fontSize=20px})
        }
        hbox(style={padding=2px}) {
            text(value="wrapped")
        }
        scroll {
            text(value="scrolled")
        }
        stack(style={padding=3px}) {
            text(value="layered")
        }
    }
}

window {
    main
}
